      "text": "Given the polynomial equation \\(x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 9 = 0\\), we know that \\(r\\), \\(s\\), and \\(t\\) are its roots. By Vieta's formulas, we have the following relationships for the roots of the polynomial \\(x^3 + px^2 + qx + r = 0\\):\n\n1. \\(r + s + t = -p\\)\n2. \\(rs + rt + st = q\\)\n3. \\(rst = -r\\)\n\nFor the given polynomial \\(x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 9 = 0\\), we identify the coefficients as:\n- \\(p = -5\\)\n- \\(q = 6\\)\n- \\(r = -(-9) = 9\\)\n\nUsing Vieta's formulas, we can determine the sums and products of the roots:\n1. \\(r + s + t = 5\\)\n2. \\(rs + rt + st = 6\\)\n3. \\(rst = -9\\)\n\nWe need to compute \\(\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s}\\). Expressing each term with a common denominator, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s} = \\frac{rs \\cdot rs + st \\cdot tr + tr \\cdot st}{rst} = \\frac{(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2}{rst}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite \\((rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2\\) using the identity:\n\\[\n(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2 = (rs + st + tr)^2 - 2rst(rs + st + tr)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2 = 6^2 - 2 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 5 = 36 - 90 = -54\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n\\frac{(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2}{rst} = \\frac{-54}{-9} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s}\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
