      "text": "Let's solve the problem step by step. The given equation is \\(x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x = 9\\). First, we rewrite this equation in standard polynomial form:\n\n\\[x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 9 = 0.\\]\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are \\(r\\), \\(s\\), and \\(t\\). By Vieta's formulas, we know the following relationships between the roots and the coefficients of the polynomial:\n\n1. \\(r + s + t = 5\\)\n2. \\(rs + rt + st = 6\\)\n3. \\(rst = 9\\)\n\nWe need to compute \\(\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s}\\). We can rewrite this expression in terms of the roots:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s} = \\frac{rs \\cdot s}{rst} + \\frac{st \\cdot t}{rst} + \\frac{tr \\cdot r}{rst} = \\frac{r^2s^2}{rst} + \\frac{s^2t^2}{rst} + \\frac{t^2r^2}{rst} = \\frac{rs^2}{t} + \\frac{st^2}{r} + \\frac{tr^2}{s}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(rst = 9\\) and the fact that \\(rs + rt + st = 6\\), we can rewrite the expression as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{rs^2}{t} + \\frac{st^2}{r} + \\frac{tr^2}{s} = \\frac{rs \\cdot s}{t} + \\frac{st \\cdot t}{r} + \\frac{tr \\cdot r}{s} = \\frac{rs \\cdot s}{t} + \\frac{st \\cdot t}{r} + \\frac{tr \\cdot r}{s} = \\frac{(rs)^2}{t} + \\frac{(st)^2}{r} + \\frac{(tr)^2}{s}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we use the fact that \\(rs + rt + st = 6\\) and \\(rst = 9\\):\n\n\\[\n(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2 = (rs + rt + st)^2 - 2rst(r + s + t).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\n\\[\n(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2 = 6^2 - 2 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 5 = 36 - 90 = -54.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{rs^2}{t} + \\frac{st^2}{r} + \\frac{tr^2}{s} = -54.\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-54}.\n\\]",
